<BaseLayout title="About LiquidityPool.app" description="LiquidityPool.app is an independent, educational research library explaining the mechanics and risks of decentralized liquidity.">
  <section class="page-hero"><div class="container"><div class="eyebrow eyebrow--mint">About the publication</div><h1 class="page-title">A research library for a market that moves too fast.</h1><p>LiquidityPool.app exists to make decentralized liquidity easier to reason about—without pretending that complexity, risk, or uncertainty can be wished away.</p></div></section>
  <section class="section section--paper"><div class="container manifesto"><p class="lead">Good research does not manufacture certainty. It gives readers a more precise way to locate uncertainty.</p><p>Liquidity pools sit at the center of much of DeFi, yet the common language around them is often compressed into yield, APR, or the latest incentive program. This publication takes the opposite approach. It begins with the mechanism: who supplies what, how prices move, where a position changes shape, and which risks remain after a headline return is removed.</p><p>The launch library is intentionally evergreen. It focuses on the concepts that recur across protocols and market cycles, then connects them with frameworks a reader can reuse. The goal is not to tell anyone what to do with capital. The goal is to make the system legible enough that a reader can ask better questions.</p><div class="principles"><div class="principle"><span>01</span><h2>Mechanism first</h2><p>We explain the moving parts before discussing outcomes. A position only makes sense in the context of the market structure that created it.</p></div><div class="principle"><span>02</span><h2>Risk is a feature</h2><p>Risk sections are not fine print. Price movement, smart-contract design, incentives, and execution belong in the main argument.</p></div><div class="principle"><span>03</span><h2>No false precision</h2><p>Where outcomes depend on changing assumptions, we name the assumptions rather than turning a scenario into a promise.</p></div></div></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
